<p class="lt-chem-13446">Let&rsquo;s combine these numbers to generate the atomic quantum numbers:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center   lt-chem-13446">\[L = 4,3,2,1,0  \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="mt-align-center   lt-chem-13446">\[M_l = 4,3,2,1,0, &#8208; 1, &#8208; 2, &#8208; 3, &#8208; 4 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="mt-align-center   lt-chem-13446">\[M_s = 1,0 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-13446">We know that there will at least one each of S, P, D, F and G. It isn&rsquo;t immediately clear which of these will be singlets and which will be triplets. To figure this out, we need to systematically examine the possible microstates. It turns out that there are <strong>45</strong> possible ways to put distribute two electrons between 5 d orbitals. That&rsquo;s a lot! The easiest way to list the states is to organize them into a chart:</p>

